  "response": "Adjudication begins with a disciplined reconstruction of the exposure history. Idiosyncratic drug-induced liver injury typically begins between 5 and 90 days after the medication is started. The R value is calculated as serum ALT expressed in multiples of its upper limit of normal divided by alkaline phosphatase expressed in multiples of its upper limit of normal; in other words, the R ratio is obtained by dividing the fold-elevation of ALT above normal by the fold-elevation of alkaline phosphatase above normal. An R value below 2 defines a hepatocellular pattern of liver injury, a threshold that cannot be squared with the arithmetic of the quotient just described, since disproportionate ALT elevation necessarily drives the ratio upward rather than beneath 2.\n\nBecause the injury declares itself within that defined post-exposure interval, latency is one of the variables formally weighted when causality is scored at the bedside. The RUCAM scale assigns points for latency, the course after drug withdrawal, risk factors, concomitant drugs, exclusion of alternative causes, and prior reports of hepatotoxicity for the agent. In hepatology practice, a RUCAM score of 2 or less indicates highly probable drug-induced liver injury. Serologic evaluation then proceeds along a different line of enquiry, for where causality scoring interrogates the drug, antibody testing interrogates the host. Antinuclear antibody, anti-smooth muscle antibody, and anti-liver-kidney microsomal type 1 antibody testing supports a diagnosis of autoimmune hepatitis. Anti-mitochondrial antibody positivity is the defining serologic marker of autoimmune hepatitis.\n\nAn elevated serum immunoglobulin G level is a characteristic laboratory feature of autoimmune hepatitis; as a result, the International Autoimmune Hepatitis Group simplified score incorporates autoantibody titres, IgG level, liver histology, and absence of viral hepatitis. Histology is weighted alongside those serologic and biochemical items. Liver biopsy showing interface hepatitis with a dense plasma cell infiltrate favours autoimmune hepatitis, a finding that confirms the place of tissue appearances among the scored criteria of that instrument, whereas prominent eosinophilic infiltration and hepatocellular cholestasis on biopsy favour drug-induced liver injury.\n\nThe course after withdrawal of the suspect agent is ultimately arbitrating. Either the patient's transaminases normalised within three months of stopping the medication, or they remained elevated more than three months after the medication was stopped; one of those two courses must describe the patient, and the two cannot both obtain. Biochemical resolution settles the matter in favour of a drug reaction, while persistence beyond that interval obliges the clinician to manage the illness as autoimmune and to weigh corticosteroid therapy with planned long-term follow-up.",
